Dear Sirs,

Again I am turning to the web knowledge, but this time I think this is for
all of us NT-Alpha-users. I am building a map of the Emulation Land, that is,
a list on the 16-bit apps that really work under NT/Alphas i286-emulation.

16-bit emulation is a controversial thing. On the other side, you would not
want to use the 16-bit apps because of the performance, but on the other hand,
some important vendors have not yet understood that world is changing. My
Digital contacts say that customers indeed want to know which apps run.
I personally think there are apps that will never be ported outside the usual
Windows arena, CD-ROMs for instance.

For some of you this might look very stoopid to make such a list. And I know,
all the apps that require a 386 processor or Windows Enhanced Mode (same
thing) do not run. The but is here: I have seen some apps run even when
the package says it requires a 386. And as well as that, some apps (Macromedia
Director 4.0 and Framemaker 4.0) install perfectly but do not run. So theres
some mystique in this. Digital says that 386-emulation is coming and the code
translator FX!32 too, but in the meanwhile, we need some kind of a solution.

I recently asked the same question in comp.os.windows.nt.misc.
Got just few, so I am sort-of reposting the question.  So if you can/want to
contribute, reply to me or to this list. I am planning to put the results
somewhere on the web if they are needed.

