From bauch@ricks.enet.dec.com Thu Nov  9 08:00:08 1995 CST
Message-ID: <9511091349.AA14901@easynet.crl.dec.com>
Date: Thu, 9 Nov 95 08:49:25 EST
From: Aaron Bauch, Alpha Strategic Marketing Manager, 225-5993, HLO2-2 <bauch@ricks.enet.dec.com>
Subject: RE: the FX!32 software...

FX!32 is based on the translation technology first delivered in the
DECmigrate toolset referred to below.  Since then it has been used for
translation from Digital's MIPs ULTRIX to Alpha OSF (now DEC UNIXtm)
and a product called Freeport Express that translated SPARC/SunOS
to Alpha/DEC UNIX.  All of the above tools were 'technical user' targetted,
meaning they were not transparent and required some user intervention
to start and manage the translation process.  The main difference in
FX!32 is that it target's the Windows end-user and therefore must run
transparently when the user installs and runs any X86 programs that it
can support.

FX!32 works only on Win32 API programs including X86 Windows NT binaries
as well as Windows95 binaries.  The new Insignia 486 emulator will continue
to be used as well to support legacy applications running Win16 APIs
like Windows3.11 and WFW.  The new 486 capability in this emulator will
enable ALL of these applications to run, as opposed to the current state
where only applications that use the 286 architecture mode are supported.

The combination of these two developments will provide an environment where
virtually ALL software just runs, and all of the interesting and performance
hungry applications (ie; Windows95 or Windows NT) will either run native
with about twice the best X86 performance, or translated by FX!32 with
up to 1.4 times the best X86 performance (your mileage may vary :<)).  Bottom
line, 1996 will be a very good year for completing the applications puzzle
for Alpha NT.

Aaron Bauch
Strategic Marketing Manager
Alpha Microprocessors
Digital Semiconductor

