From (no email) Thu Nov 16 01:41:54 1995 CST
Message-ID: <9511160741.AA15101@foo.garply.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Nov 1995 01:41:54 -0600 (CST)
From: Aaron Sakovich <(no email)>
Subject: Re: FAT partitions - why??
In-Reply-To: <199511152019.OAA22075@nasa.cca.rockwell.com>

> >I noticed when installing NT on alpha systems that for some unknown reason
> >the partition with the bootfiles cannot be anything else than FAT. 
> >
> >josef
> >******************************************************************
> >From "Windows NT Resource Guide", page 203:
> 
> CAUTION: You must not convert the boot partition to NTFS on a RISC-based
> computer.  This is because the firmware on RISC-based computers loads the
> first system files in the Windows NT boot process, and the firmware understands
> only the FAT file system.

Well, that's only part of the story.  The SYSTEM partition needs to be
FAT.  The BOOT partition can be NTFS.  The nomenclature here is *backwards*
from what most people might expect -- the system partition can be a small
(e.g. 4 MB) area that holds the NTLDR and other initialization software.
The boot partition is that area that you would expect the \WINNT351 directory,
PAGEFILE, \USERS, \WIN32APP, and other conventional access files.  This area
can be NTFS.

The way to accomplish this is to run ARCINST.EXE (you can do it from the ARC
console "Run a program..." prompt by specifying CDROM:\ALPHA\ARCINST.EXE I
believe).  Then, create 2 partitions -- the first primary partition being
the 4 MB area, and the second the remainder of the disk.  You'll then get your
prompt for converting your boot partition to NTFS.

The FAT system partition is secured by the operating system during the
installation to prevent the files from being inadvertantly modified.

Aaron

