Great Jim, I'm glad you got that worked out.   On the home directory 
issue, there are many creative ways to handle this.   From a logon 
script the "net use <drive letter> /home" should work.  Also, start 
thinking about the power of shortcuts....you can have a shortcut on a 
desktop (and what's on the desktop can be part of profiles).   That 
shortcut could be a home directory or it could be a folder that is a 
folder of shortcuts.   An adminitrator could maintain a standard folder 
of shortcuts, that points to all the places people need to get to.   We 
are trying to move away from the concept of "redirected DOS drives" to 
UNC (universal naming convention) names.  So a working directory can be 
\\a_server\a_share\%username%\wordfiles instead of h:\wordfiles.  Also 
review how you can use Windows 95 user profiles....... so there are 
lot's of options.

But even with all these options I do wish we did have a plain old "map 
root" command with Microsoft networking.  I have requested that we add 
that feature....but as I said, I think the main reason for that is that 
we are trying to move away from the limitations of DOS and 26 letters.

Hope this helps
