From (no email) Mon Nov 20 02:30:06 1995 CST
Message-ID: <9511200830.AA09432@foo.garply.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Nov 1995 02:30:06 -0600 (CST)
From: Aaron Sakovich <(no email)>
Subject: Re: Alpha NT Performance woes
In-Reply-To: <199511181813.MAA02036@foghorn.cc.utexas.edu>

> Installed msvc20 and started compiling (the main reason I got an alpha).
> 
> Just to see hte speed diffrence I started the exact same compile (about 30 files)
> on my Pentium 90.  Exact same flags etc.  The performance was exactly the same.
> The alpha finished 4-5 seconds ahead of the pentium out of a total of about 10 minutes.

The Alpha finished *faster* than the Pentium?  Wow, I'm surprised!!!  I've
seen others reporting that compile times were up to 8x that of an Intel box.

This is to be totally expected behavior -- if anyone sold you a RISC processor
claiming that compile times were faster, you were done a great dis-service.
Compilers on RISC machines are incredibly more complex than those on CISC
processors (like a Pentium).  The RISC environment requires much more care
in ordering and optimizing instruction execution for branch prediction, 
minimizing pipeline flushes, etc.  Remember, as I and others have said before 
here and other places, RISC stands for Relegate Important Stuff to the Compiler.

> Also the resulting executable runs about 10% faster in a totally compute bound operation.

Try other optimization settings.  Unlike CISC processors, you cannot assume
that a specific optimization style will result in better performance.  With
a RISC machine, they are just different styles, and different code will have
different results independent of the "O#".  As a general rule, your machine
should smoke the P90 by at least a factor of 2 for integer work, more for
fp intensive stuff.

YMMV...

Aaron

