From LJKpublish@eworld.com Wed Nov 22 19:04:39 1995 CST
Message-ID: <951122170341_19065128@hp1.online.apple.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Nov 1995 17:03:41 -0800
From: LJKpublish@eworld.com <LJKpublish@eworld.com>
Subject: Re: More on FX32

tsharma@dialcall.com (Sharma, Tushar) writes:

> Curiously, these numbers belong to Semiconductor Marketing Group at DEC.

Actually, that is not so curious.  Those folks are the ones interested
in selling Alpha chips, regardless of which manufacturer's box they go
into.  They apparently have the notion that the way to get manufacturers
to use Alpha chips in their product is to increase end-user demand for
products containing Alpha chips.

In this case, the push is by getting better performance for software
whose vendors have not built an Alpha-specific version.  Of course
a competitor who builds an Alpha-specific version can always make
something which performs better than the FX!32'd version.

In a previous incarnation, the technical folks behind DEC translation
technology did the VEST software which converted VAX/VMS
applications to Alpha VMS.  They had a similar offering for Unix.
The need for VEST has mostly gone away, as any vendor who
has updated their VMS application in the last two years has built
a native Alpha version.  Most folks are not interested in applications
which do not update their feature set regularly.  Presumably DEC is
hoping for the same thing on Windows NT.

Some people are interested in staying with Intel chips forever.  Others
who are considering RISC may be swayed to whichever RISC chip
gives them best performance on applications not compiled for RISC.
Presumably all RISC chips give better performance than Intel on
applications custom-built for RISC.

